# Data Processing Guidelines

## When to Check These Rules
- When implementing business logic
- When processing complex data operations
- When handling data validation
- When implementing caching strategies
- When working with service objects
- When logging important operations

## Business Logic
- Keep business logic in models
- Use service objects for complex operations
- Implement proper validation
- Handle edge cases explicitly
- Document complex operations
- Use appropriate design patterns
- Consider performance implications
- Implement proper error handling

## Service Objects
- Use service objects for complex operations
- Follow single responsibility principle
- Implement proper error handling
- Use clear, descriptive method names
- Document service object behavior
- Consider transaction boundaries
- Handle edge cases explicitly
- Implement proper logging

## Caching
- Use appropriate caching strategies
- Consider cache invalidation
- Implement proper cache keys
- Handle cache misses gracefully
- Monitor cache performance
- Consider cache size limits
- Implement proper cache cleanup
- Document caching strategies

## Examples

✅ Correct:
```ruby
# Service object for complex operation
class UserRegistrationService
  def initialize(params)
    @params = params
  end

  def call
    ActiveRecord::Base.transaction do
      user = create_user
      send_welcome_email(user)
      create_user_profile(user)
      log_registration(user)
    end
  rescue StandardError => e
    logger.error "Registration failed: #{e.message}"
    raise
  end

  private

  def create_user
    User.create!(@params)
  end

  def send_welcome_email(user)
    UserMailer.welcome_email(user).deliver_later
  end

  def create_user_profile(user)
    UserProfile.create!(user: user)
  end

  def log_registration(user)
    logger.info "User registered: #{user.id}"
  end
end

# Proper caching
class User < ApplicationRecord
  def cached_posts
    Rails.cache.fetch("user/#{id}/posts", expires_in: 1.hour) do
      posts.to_a
    end
  end
end
```

❌ Incorrect:
- Business logic in controllers
- No service objects for complex operations
- Poor error handling
- No proper validation
- No caching strategy
- No proper logging
- No edge case handling
- No transaction management

## Common Mistakes to Avoid
- Mixing business logic in controllers
- Not using service objects for complex operations
- Poor error handling
- No proper validation
- No caching strategy
- No proper logging
- No edge case handling
- No transaction management
- Not considering performance
- Not documenting complex operations

## Validation Checklist
- [ ] Business logic is in models
- [ ] Service objects are used for complex operations
- [ ] Proper validation is implemented
- [ ] Edge cases are handled
- [ ] Appropriate caching is used
- [ ] Proper logging is implemented
- [ ] Error handling is in place
- [ ] Performance is considered
- [ ] Complex operations are documented
- [ ] Transaction boundaries are proper
